#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:2
msgid "Bibliography and Citations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:4
msgid ""
"typsphinx renders bibliographies and citations by rendering "
"`sphinxcontrib-bibtex <https://pypi.org/project/sphinxcontrib-"
"bibtex/>`_'s *resolved* citation nodes as ordinary Typst content. That is"
" the defining guarantee for this feature: typsphinx carries **zero** "
"bibliography-specific code. By the time a document reaches the Typst "
"translator, sphinxcontrib-bibtex has already turned every ``:cite:`` role"
" and every ``.. bibliography::`` directive into plain docutils citation, "
"reference and inline nodes, and the generic visitors that already existed"
" handle them. Nothing in the pipeline knows what a BibTeX key is."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:14
msgid ""
"The practical consequence is that what sphinxcontrib-bibtex supports is "
"what typsphinx supports, and the constraints you can trip are its "
"constraints rather than typsphinx's."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:19
msgid "Setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:21
msgid ""
"``sphinxcontrib-bibtex`` is **not** a typsphinx dependency. typsphinx "
"itself carries only Sphinx, docutils and Typst at runtime, so you install"
" the bibliography extension yourself::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:27
msgid ""
"Then enable it in your own ``conf.py`` alongside typsphinx, and point it "
"at your ``.bib`` files:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:39
msgid ""
"No typsphinx setting needs to change. There is no bibliography-related "
"configuration value in typsphinx, because there is no bibliography-"
"related code path to configure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:44
msgid "What Is Supported"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:46
msgid ""
"Every form below is exercised end to end by the test suite, in both the "
"emitted ``.typ`` markup and the compiled PDF."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:50
msgid "Parenthetical Citations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:52
msgid ""
"``:cite:p:`` renders as a bracketed citation group, the usual "
"``[1]``-style parenthetical reference:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:60
msgid "Textual Citations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:62
msgid ""
"``:cite:t:`` renders the author name *outside* the bracket group, so the "
"citation reads as part of the sentence:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:70
msgid "Multi-Key Citations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:72
msgid ""
"A single role naming several keys renders as **one** bracketed group, not"
" as one group per key:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:80
msgid "Footnote Citations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:82
msgid ""
"``:footcite:`` paired with ``.. footbibliography::`` renders as real "
"Typst footnotes, printed at the foot of the page where the citing site "
"appears -- not collected into a list at the end of the document:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:92
msgid ""
"Citing the same key a second time emits a bare reuse reference rather "
"than a second footnote body, so the entry text is not duplicated on the "
"page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:96
msgid "Reference Lists"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:98
msgid ""
"``.. bibliography::`` renders as a Typst ``grid``: one row per entry, "
"each row carrying the pybtex label and the formatted entry body."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:108
msgid ""
"A bare directive renders only the entries actually cited somewhere in the"
" project, not every entry in your ``.bib`` files. Add the ``:all:`` "
"option if you want the whole file listed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:113
msgid "Several Reference Lists in One Project"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:115
msgid ""
"Multiple ``.. bibliography::`` directives render as independent grids. A "
"common shape is one chapter-local list narrowed with ``:filter:`` plus a "
"corpus-wide list at the end of the project; each renders its own rows, "
"and neither interferes with the other. See the first constraint below for"
" how that interacts with an entry two lists both own."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:122
msgid "Citations Across the ``#include()`` Boundary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:124
msgid ""
"A citing site in an included document resolves against a ``.. "
"bibliography::`` living in a *different* document, and the resulting link"
" survives the Typst ``#include()`` boundary into the compiled PDF. This "
"works for the ordinary layout in which a master document is a pure "
"toctree, every chapter is an included file, and the reference list sits "
"in its own chapter -- so every single citation has to cross an include "
"boundary to reach its entry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:132
msgid "Constraints You Can Trip"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:134
msgid ""
"The three constraints below are measured behaviour, not theory. Each is "
"the behaviour of sphinxcontrib-bibtex that typsphinx faithfully passes "
"through."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:138
msgid "Entries Appear Once Per Reference List, Not Once Per Document"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:140
msgid ""
"Exactly-once is a *per reference list* property. An entry owned by two "
"``.. bibliography::`` directives -- say a chapter-local filtered list and"
" a corpus-wide list -- legitimately renders **twice** in the finished "
"PDF, once in each grid. That is correct output, not a duplication bug."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:145
msgid ""
"On the test suite's own four-entry fixture the measured entry-body "
"occurrence counts in the compiled PDF are 1, 2, 1 and 0: the ``2`` is the"
" key that two bibliographies both own, and the ``0`` is an entry nothing "
"cites. If you are writing your own check over generated output, count per"
" grid rather than per document."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:152
msgid "The Key-on-Left ``:filter:`` Form Degrades Silently"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:154
msgid "``:filter:`` expressions must put the key *string* on the left:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:161
msgid ""
"Writing the comparison the other way round -- ``key == \"Smith2020\"`` --"
" is a **silent** failure. The grid simply empties, while the build still "
"exits 0 with **zero warnings, even under** ``-W``. The citing sites do "
"not warn either, because they fall back to a corpus-wide bibliography "
"elsewhere in the project, so sphinxcontrib-bibtex's "
"``bibtex.key_not_found`` never fires."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:167
msgid ""
"Build cleanliness therefore cannot detect this. If you use ``:filter:``, "
"check the *rendered* output -- look at the grid and confirm the rows you "
"expect are present. A clean strict build is not evidence that the filter "
"matched anything."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:172
msgid "``:keyprefix:`` Is Load-Bearing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:174
msgid ""
"When the same key is rendered by two bibliographies in one project, the "
"second one needs ``:keyprefix:`` to keep its anchors distinct:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:183
msgid ""
"This is not cosmetic. Removing ``:keyprefix:`` in that situation is a "
"hard ``bibtex.duplicate_citation`` failure under a strict (``-W``) build."
" The prefix stays internal to the anchors -- it does not leak into the "
"rendered labels or entry bodies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:189
msgid "Citation Style Is Owned By pybtex"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:191
msgid ""
"Citation labels and the formatting of each entry body come from `pybtex "
"<https://pypi.org/project/pybtex/>`_, selected through sphinxcontrib-"
"bibtex's ``bibtex_default_style``. typsphinx does not reformat them: "
"whatever string pybtex produced for a label or an entry body is emitted "
"into the Typst output as-is."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:197
msgid ""
"In particular, **Typst's own** ``#bibliography()`` **and** ``#cite()`` "
"**functions are deliberately not used.** A reference list is emitted as a"
" ``grid`` of already-formatted rows, and a citation as an already-"
"formatted reference. The consequences are worth stating plainly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:202
msgid ""
"Typst's CSL styles are **unavailable**. Supplying a ``.csl`` file has no "
"effect on typsphinx output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:204
msgid ""
"Typst never sees your ``.bib`` files. It sees only text that pybtex "
"already rendered."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:206
msgid ""
"To change how citations and entries look, configure pybtex and "
"sphinxcontrib-bibtex -- not Typst."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/user_guide/bibliography.rst:209
msgid ""
"This is the same choice Sphinx's own LaTeX builder makes: it emits "
"pybtex-rendered ``\\bibitem`` entries rather than handing the ``.bib`` "
"file to BibTeX. Keeping the same ownership boundary is what lets the "
"Typst, LaTeX and HTML builders agree on labels, bodies and ordering for "
"the same sources, which the test suite asserts as a three-way comparison."
msgstr ""

